One note on the brief: its baseline section says the gain has to come from 1024 and 256, while the task restricts the class to what aliases at 32/16 — the round was carried out as written, and the physical fix paid at every size.

Two in-class hypotheses were measured and killed (our grain is *quieter* than C2's at every band that survives downsampling; the 32px edge deficit is coarse silhouette offset, not small-size damage). The real defect: our ribbon sat a mean **0.388 L below the ground it covers**, C2's sits 0.05–0.11 below. Its interior read p10 0.319/median 0.338 against C2's bore at 0.672/0.737 — the roll was drawn as an opaque tube, and the one transmitted term was gated on `max(0, lam)`, so it evaluated to exactly zero on the deep bands it existed to rescue.

Three named constants replace it: `CURL_TRANSMIT 0.42`, `CURL_BORE 0.54`, `CURL_SHEEN 0.22`, all fitted to C2's measured interior. Ribbon p10 0.414→**0.592** (C2 0.590), p90 0.780→**0.816** (0.821), below-ground-by-0.35 share 5.9%→**0.0%**. Zero bytes, zero paths added.

Rubric: the #4 deduction — the curl's small-size noise — is engaged for the first time and its own quantity halves (16px mean |ΔL| 0.0806→0.0433, worst pixel 0.2252→0.0891); it now reads as a pale note, not a dark blot. Figure-ground, the 16px read and the single light model all hold. Costs: 256 edge_f1 −0.0149 and one 16px edge pixel, from the ribbon's range compressing 0.366→0.224 — toward C2's own 0.231.

**Reusable:** *a thin translucent material has a floor, and the floor does not fall off with depth.* Model transmission as three terms (far-side lambert, bore floor, ambient sheen), lerp toward the transmission colour **after** occlusion rather than multiplying, and diagnose with "how far below the ground it covers does this form ever fall?" A term switched off everywhere it matters is indistinguishable from an absent one — only sampling the render tells you which you have.

